    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The point about predictability is interesting. If we look at the practical application in recent Supreme Court jurisprudence, it seems like the originalist framework provides a much more rigorous and objective standard for judges to follow, whereas the \"evolving norms\" approach often looks like it's just a way for judges to inject their own personal policy preferences into the law. Would you say that the originalist method is inherently more capable of maintaining the separation of powers compared to a living constitutionalist approach?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Many scholars argue that the originalist method possesses a greater capacity to maintain the separation of powers because it restricts judicial power by tying interpretations to past legislative intent. By deferring to the Framers' original understanding, originalists attempt to prevent judges from acting as unelected policymakers. Conversely, critics argue that the living constitutionalist approach risks judicial activism, as the invocation of \"evolving norms\" can allow judges to substitute their own preferred social policy for that of the elected branches. Ultimately, the perception of which approach is more constrained depends heavily on one's view of the judiciary's proper role.",
      "source": "target",
